From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Choose the streamed mode from both ends of the link

Every session logged "No resolution found, falling back to standard FullHD
resolution" and streamed 1920x1080 at 30 Hz to every receiver, whatever it
could take and whatever was being shared. The selection exists upstream but
is disabled behind #if 0, with a comment that the native resolution some
devices report is useless. That concerns the "native" field, which is still
ignored here.

Three facts are available and all three are now used.

What the receiver accepts. Its advertised modes are its own statement. A
receiver advertises three tables: CEA holds the timings consumer televisions
are built to display, VESA the computer monitor ones, HH the handheld ones.
get_resolutions() flattens all three, which loses the only information
saying what kind of display is listening: ranking across the flattened list
sent a television 1920x1200, a monitor shape it accepted but had to
resynchronise for, and the picture flashed. CEA modes are preferred when a
receiver offers any.

What is being shared. The portal reports the size of the captured screen or
window, which is passed to the client. Nothing larger is considered: sending
more pixels than exist spends bandwidth and encoding time on an upscale the
receiver could do itself.

How much of it a mode actually shows. The picture is fitted without
distortion, so a mode of a different shape wastes the difference on black
bars. Ranking by the fitted scale accounts for that without reasoning about
shape separately: 1920x1080 shows more of a 16:10 screen than the same-shaped
1680x1050 does, while a 4:3 screen is better served by 1600x1200 than by
1920x1080.

Ties fall back to the existing compare_resolutions(). No preference between
a sharper picture and a smoother one is expressed anywhere: which is better
depends on the room, the screen and what is being shown, and neither end
states a preference. Interlaced modes are skipped because the pipeline
produces progressive frames, and the 1080p30 fallback remains for receivers
that advertise nothing usable.

The choice is a function of its own so it can be tested against real
advertised lists without a client, a socket or a television.

